Thanks very much Andy and Evan for the quick and helpful replies. I see that there is much I need to review/learn yet. (My head is not quite wrapping around all the concepts, but it eventually sticks if I keep hammering.)

The “infinity” problem seems especially likely when species have low sample size and small sigma relative to distance bins, and probably inadequate covariates to explain variation in abundance (which I assume causes abundance and phi to be confounded).  But even just working with relatively common and straightforward species, I have many cases where the negative binomial models perform better than poisson according to AIC, which I assume means overdispersion (not surprising), and in many of these cases, the poisson model tends to predict phi at 1 (with CI 0 to 1), while the negative binomial model predicts a small phi (with wide CI) and inflated abundance.  And if I increase K to a huge # it pushes phi toward 0 and abundance toward infinity... sigh.

But for the simulation example that I gave earlier, it doesn’t seem possible to predict the lambda CI for sites with abundance 0, no matter how large the sample size is (at other sites), and no matter how easy it is to estimate the few parameters involved, and no matter if using gdistsamp or stacked in distsamp.  (Unless I cheat by adding a single detection.)  I assume that this is a simple/obvious limitation of “predict” that is unavoidable?

I have a ways to go in just learning the basic mechanics involved here, but still very grateful for any tips.
Thanks,
Ed

