Dear All,

 I am working on ungulate data from Colombian Guiana forest with Royle y Nichols approach. We developed the sampling in two protected area, one with continue Amazon forest and the second with Riparian forest. We make 15 lineal transect about 3 to 5 km by long, detecting species (tracks, direct observation) during consecutive days in April and July of the same year.

 We divide each line transect into spatial subunits of equal length (1.1 km) obtained 48 sites.

 We have 12 detection histories for 48 sites, distributed in two study areas (2 National Park).

 The subunits along the same transect in each study area are spatially depend and are not independent.

 Originally we used RN model to find main factors (% cover in a buffer, structural vegetation, etc.) influencing “abundance” considering all the sites in Unmarked.

 However, we are unsure on the validity of our approach since we do not include any explicit spatial processes about the block (transect) on the modeling procedure.

 We think include the transect as factor in abundance RN model in unmarked.

 But, reading the new Book of Kery & Royle, maybe it better include this random factor in a Bayesian approach.

 I appreciate any opinion on the best way to go ahead and any advice to a valid approach to include the spatial relations between spatial subunits by transect (block)?

Thanks in advance,

